 "productionNotes": "The per-mark figures are consistent across the sources consulted and derive ultimately from the factory ledgers as published in Clausinger's restorer's guide. Mark I: 13,650 cars, comprising 2,825 BN7 two-seaters and 10,825 BT7 2+2s. Mark II: 11,564 cars, comprising 355 BN7s, 5,096 BT7s and 6,113 BJ7 sports convertibles. Mark III: 17,712 BJ8s. That totals 42,926, the figure given by Wikipedia citing Clausinger and by the Healey Drivers Club, and the component numbers are repeated independently by the Austin-Healey Club of America's Big Healey guide and by healey.org. Looser approximations circulate in secondary writing and do not reconcile with the breakdown: Ate Up With Motor gives 'approximately 44,000' for the 3000 models, and Classics World describes the 3000 as roughly 60 per cent of nearly 79,000 Big Healeys, which would imply something closer to 47,000. Neither is presented as a ledger figure and neither is preferred here. The 355 Mark II BN7s make that car the rarest series-production Austin-Healey. Within the Mark III the split either side of the May 1964 Phase II chassis revision is contested: healey.org states 1,390 Phase I cars and 16,322 Phase II, while Conceptcarz states 1,302 Phase I. Both agree the Mark III total is 17,712. Roughly nine cars in ten were exported, with the Healey Drivers Club recording only 2,725 3000s sold new in the United Kingdom and 80 per cent of output going to North America. Build details for an individual car can be confirmed against the British Motor Industry Heritage Trust ledgers, which cover Austin-Healey from 1953 to 1971.",
